als, HPC benchmarks), delivering a median 1.41× / geometric-mean 2.05× end-to-end speedup, every number reproducible against the application's own production GPU code.\nL225: ## 🧭 Architecture\nL226: \nL227:     flowchart LR\nL228:       subgraph KA[\"Kernel Agent\"]\nL229:         direction LR\nL230:         P[Plan] --> C[Code] --> Pr[Profile] --> P\nL231:       end\nL232:       subgraph AA[\"App Agent\"]\nL233:         direction LR\nL234:         H[Locate hotspot] --> F[\"Forge app-specific operator<br/>(knowledge-base-driven)\"] --> V[Verify correctness] --> I[Integrate]\nL235:       end\nL236:       KA -->|optimized operators| LIB[(\"kernel/<br/>operator library =<br/>knowledge base\")]\nL237:       LIB -->|techniques & operators| AA\nL238:       AA -->|\"single USE_OURS switch\"| HR[\"Harness:<br/>interleaved measure vs<br/>the app's own GPU code\"]\nL239:       HR -->|validated speedup| RES[(\"results<br/>registry\")]\nL240: Loading\nL241: ## 📑 Contents\nL242: \nL243: cite98†Highlights · cite99†Why ForgeStencil? · cite100†Quick Start · cite101†Results · cite102†How it works · cite103†Repository layout · cite104†FAQ · cite105†Limitations · cite106†Contributing · cite107†Roadmap · cite108†Acknowledgments · cite109†License · cite110†Citation L244: \nL245: * * *\nL246: ## ✨ Highlights\nL247:   * 🤖 Two-agent loop — a Kernel Agent researches & generates CUDA stencil operators (Plan → Code → Profile), an App Agent forges app-specific operators on top of this knowledge base and integrates them into real applications.\nL248:   * 🔬 Research, not just codegen — the agent discovers optimization strategies (tiling, fusion, layout, occupancy, host-side restructuring), it doesn't just search a fixed space.\nL249:   * 🏭 Real applications, not benchmarks — the baseline is the app's own production GPU code, not a simplified reference.\nL250:   * 🔒 Auditable measurement protocol — single in-program switch, program's own correctness check, interleaved median, geometric mean over all standard cases. Faking a speedup becomes a system-level error.\nL251:   * 📦 Patch-mode, license-clean — no third-party source is bundled; each app ships a provenance record + fetch script + our integration patch.\nL252:   * 🧬 Multi-architecture — one operator library with runtime dispatch for A100 (sm_80), H100 (sm_90) and B200 (sm_100); generation-specific re-forge wins are arch-gated so other paths stay byte-identical. See cite111†the cross-generation study .\nL253:   * ♻️ Reproducible — every reported number is re-runnable from a fresh clone (verified end-to-end on A100).\nL254: * * *\nL255: ## 🆚 Why ForgeStencil?\nL256: \nL257: Stencil optimization has been automated for two decades — but only the code generation. Humans still design the optimization strategy and do the integration. ForgeStencil automates the strategy discovery and the deployment.\nL258: Capability  | DSL / Codegen\nL259: (Halide, Devito)  | Autotuners\nL260: (AN5D, EBISU, DRStencil)  | ForgeStencil\nL261: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL262: Generate optimized kernel code  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅\nL263: Discover new optimization strategies  | ❌ (human-designed)  | ⚠️ (search within a fixed space)  | ✅ (agent explores)\nL264: Deploy into a real application (hotspot → forge → integrate)  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅\nL265: Verify correctness inside the real program  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅\nL266: Auditable e2e speedup vs the app's own production GPU code  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅\nL267: \nL268: * * *\nL269: ## 🚀 Quick Start\nL270: \nL271: Requirements: an NVIDIA GPU (validated on A100-SXM4-80GB, `sm_80`), CUDA 12.x, a C++17 host compiler, Python 3.9+ with `numpy` + `cupy`, and a `git` with HTTPS support (a minimal conda `git` may lack the `https` helper — use `/usr/bin/git`).\nL272: \nL273:     git clone <this-repo> forge-stencil && cd forge-stencil\nL274:     python -c \"import numpy, cupy; print('deps OK')\"     # verify install\nL275: \nL276: 🤖 Using a coding agent (e.g. Claude Code)?\nL277: Paste this to your agent to set up and run the first demo automatically:\nL278: \nL279: > Read the README of `<this-repo>` (raw URL), then from a clone run the Quick Start operator demo `python tools/run.py --stencil star_1 --shape 256` on an idle GPU and report the measured speedup vs the Halide baseline.\nL280: ### 1. Measure an operator in ~1 minute (no app download needed)\nL281: \nL282: The fastest way to see ForgeStencil work — builds our kernel and measures it (baselines are optional; absent ones show as `null`):\nL283: \nL284:     python tools/run.py --stencil star_1 --shape 256 --gpu 0\nL285: Expected output\nL286: \nL287:     `  [1/1] star_1 256³ ...\nL288:       0.074 ms  [PASS]                     # our kernel, verified vs NumPy reference\nL289:       === Halide (single-step) ===\nL290:       Stencil   Shape     AMReX(ms)  Ours(ms)   BL(ms)   BL(GC/s)   Status\nL291:       star_1    256³           N/A     0.074     0.144    116.63    [PASS]\nL292:       Correctness: ALL PASSED\nL293:     `\nL294: \nL295: (Here our kernel is 0.074 ms vs Halide 0.144 ms; AMReX/other baselines are optional — see cite112†`baselines/` .)\nL296: ### 2. Reproduce a full end-to-end application result\nL297: \nL298: Fetch the real upstream, apply our patch, and re-measure with the app's own correctness check and timer:\nL299: \nL300:     cd applications/haccmk\nL301:     ./vendor.sh                                 # fetch upstream at the recorded version\nL302:     git apply patches/integration.patch         # our USE_OURS injection (from this app dir)\nL303:     python ../../harness/run_e2e.py --app haccmk --gpu auto\nL304: Expected output\nL305: \nL306:     `[e2e] build [orig] ...  build [ours] ...\nL307:     [e2e]   round 1/7: orig=0.0027s  ours=0.0001s\nL308:     [e2e]   ...\nL309:     [e2e] app-level correctness: True  (builtin check PASS)\nL310:     [e2e] MEASURED end-to-end GEOMEAN speedup over 1 case(s): 36.703x\nL311:     `\nL312: \nL313: (Verified from a fresh clone on A100. The absolute ratio varies with GPU/node state; correctness is invariant.)\nL314: > Run any of the 100 apps the same way — see cite113†`applications/README.md` . To drive the agents themselves, see cite114†`agents/README.md` .\nL315: \nL316: * * *\nL317: ## 📊 Results (audited)\nL318: \nL319: All figures come directly from the result registry (`results/integration_registry.json`) and are reproducible via the harness. We report the full distribution.\nL320: \nL321: ### End-to-end, across 100 validated applications\nL322: \nL323: Metric  | Value\nL324: --- | ---\nL325: Median end-to-end speedup  | 1.41×\nL326: Geometric mean  | 2.05×\nL327: Range  | 0.998× – 97.7×\nL328: ≥ 1.05×  | 89%\nL329: ≥ 1.20×  | 73%\nL330: ≥ 1.50×  | 43%\nL331: ≥ 3×  | 21%\nL332: ≥ 10×  | 7%\nL333: ### Operator-level performance\nL334: \nL335: Roofline (baseline-independent). Measured with Nsight Compute, ForgeStencil's memory-bound stencil kernels reach 74–85% of the A100's 2039 GB/s peak DRAM bandwidth — near the practical hardware ceiling for bandwidth-bound stencils. (`box_2` and `diamond_ts4` are compute/reuse-bound and sit in a different regime.)\nL336: vs the best baseline at each shape — at the same precision. At every (stencil, shape) we take the strongest applicable public result, comparing like with like: single-step vs single-step (Halide, Devito), temporal-blocking vs temporal-blocking (EBISU), and fp16 vs fp16 — where `star_1`/`star_2` use fp16 on cubic shapes, Halide is run at fp16 too.\nL337: Stencil (precision)  | Cases  | Geomean vs best baseline  | Leads on\nL338: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL339: `star_1` (7-pt, fp16/fp32)  | 9  | 1.63×  | 9 / 9†\nL340: `star_2` (13-pt, fp16/fp32)  | 7  | 1.92×  | 7 / 7\nL341: `box_1` (27-pt, fp32)  | 5  | 3.08×  | 5 / 5\nL342: `box_2` (fp32)  | 3  | 4.60×  | 3 / 3\nL343: `diamond_1` (fp32)  | 3  | 3.08×  | 3 / 3\nL344: `varcoeff_star_1` (fp32)  | 5  | 1.51×  | 5 / 5\nL345: Overall  | 32  | ≈2.16×  | 32 / 32\nL346: Numbers are the median of 7 robust passes; the bar is set by both Halide and Devito. Crucially, `star_1`/`star_2` win even when Halide runs at the same fp16 precision (fp16-vs-fp16: 1.75× over the 8 cubic shapes) — the advantage is a genuine same-precision implementation win, not a precision artifact (Halide barely benefits from fp16; our kernels exploit the halved bandwidth). On temporal-blocking stencils vs EBISU (temporal blocking): `diamond_ts4` 1.82× (win), `diamond_ts8` 0.73× (loss).\nL347: †`star_1` 128³ is an exact tie (1.00×); all other shapes lead. Reproduce with `tools/run.py` after fetching baselines via cite112†`baselines/vendor_baselines.sh` .\nL348: ### Across GPU generations (A100 → H100 → B200)\nL349: \nL350: The operator library carries runtime architecture dispatch (sm_80 / sm_90 / sm_100): the same `kernel/` sources pick arch-gated variants where a generation-specific re-forge won, and the A100 paths stay byte-identical otherwise. The cross-generation study asked: do agent-forged kernels transfer, and when does re-forging pay?\nL351: Transition  | What the HW relaxed  | Outcome  | Re-forge capture\nL352: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL353: A100 → H100 (BW +1.68×)  | compute, barely bandwidth  | kernels arrive still bandwidth-saturated (DRAM 77–90%) → near-optimal transfer  | `box_2` (compute-bound): +6–7% via config re-tuning (kernel routing + launch bounds)\nL354: H100 → B200 (BW +2.4×)  | bandwidth ≫ memory-issue rate  | kernels fall off the roofline (DRAM 78–90% → 44–65%), B200/H100 geomean 1.51× vs the 2.39× bandwidth ratio  | `box_1`: structural deep-prefetch, +7.0% @512³ / +5.1% @1024³\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[CAKE] Long-term CAKE-generated Kernel Progress Tracker · Issue #4254 · flashinfer-ai/flashinfer · GitHub (https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 688\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2608.20711] AsmEvo: Agentic Assembly-Level Optimization of AMD GPU Kernels with Functional Equivalence Verification (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20711)\nciteturn9view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20711\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\n"
